Chapter: GopFont
  cites Works chapter GopFat16
  cites Guios chapter FontLoad
  cites Guios chapter GuiDisplay

 We say:

Section: Font Handle

 `gf-gh` is the cell, and a cell is not a place to put text. Two more rows
 say where inside it the type actually sits: `gf-asc` is the distance from
 the top of the cell down to the BASELINE, and `gf-cap` is the height of a
 capital above that baseline. A caller centring a line of capitals in a box
 wants the cap band, which runs from `gf-asc - gf-cap` to `gf-asc`, and it
 is the only band a reader perceives as the text. Centring the CELL instead
 sits the type low by half the descent, which is what an all-caps label in a
 button showed the moment the cell grew to the face's real box.

  GopFont = record {
    gf-ok : Boolean,
    gf-base : Integer,
    gf-gw : Integer,
    gf-gh : Integer,
    gf-asc : Integer,
    gf-cap : Integer
  }

  gfont-none : GopFont
  gfont-none = GopFont { gf-ok = False, gf-base = 0, gf-gw = 0, gf-gh = 0, gf-asc = 0, gf-cap = 0 }

Section: Load

 One flat read, then the guios pipeline verbatim. The glyph pixel
 lists and supersampling scratch this allocates live until the
 caller's heap mark is restored; a desktop session loads once at
 entry and reclaims at exit.

 The face is rasterized AT the size it will be drawn, because `gbf-put-text`
 has no scale factor: it paints the glyph bitmaps it was given. A 16ppem face
 on a display the widget layer scales by two therefore draws at half the size
 of the bitmap fallback beside it, which is what the desk showed while only
 its hand-drawn prose used this path. Passing the scaled ppem is also the
 better of the two ways to get there, since the rasterizer hints at the
 target size rather than magnifying a small raster.

 `gf-gh` is the CELL height and not the ppem, and the two differ. A face's
 bounding box may exceed its em, so cmunss at a 32 pixel em rasterizes into
 41 rows (`FontLoad`, `fb-render-glyph`). Handing `ppem` to
 `fl-raster-to-gobf` therefore stored 32 of those 41 rows and cut the
 descenders off at the bottom of the box; the height comes off the rasters
 now, which is what `fl-max-height` was written for and was never called
 for. A caller wanting the type size still passes `ppem` in; a caller
 placing a line reads `gf-gh`.

  gfont-load : Fat16Volume, Text, Integer -> [Device.Port] GopFont
  gfont-load (vol) (name) (ppem) = act
    size <- gfat-file-size vol name
    if size == 0 then gfont-none
    else act
      let buf = __heap-save
      in let adv = __heap-advance (size + 1024)
      in act
        n <- gfat-read-file-bulk vol name buf
        if n == 0 then gfont-none
        else if fb-ttf-plausible buf n then
          let font = fb-parse-ttf buf size
          in let rasters = fb-render-ascii buf font ppem
          in let maxw = fl-max-width rasters 0 0
          in let maxh = fl-max-height rasters 0 0
          in let gbase = fl-raster-to-gobf rasters maxw maxh
          in let upem = font.tf-head.th-units-per-em
          in let asc = font.tf-head.th-y-max * ppem / upem
          in GopFont { gf-ok = True, gf-base = gbase, gf-gw = maxw, gf-gh = maxh, gf-asc = asc, gf-cap = fl-cap-rows buf font ppem }
        else gfont-none
      end
    end
  end

Section: Draw

  gfont-text : GopFont, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Text, Integer -> Integer
  gfont-text (f) (base) (stride) (h) (x) (y) (s) (fg) =
    gbf-put-text (GopBuf { gb-base = base, gb-width = stride, gb-height = h }) (f.gf-base) (f.gf-gw) (f.gf-gh) x y s fg

 The face is PROPORTIONAL: `fl-raster-to-gobf` writes a 95-entry advance table
 after the glyph bitmaps, and `gbf-put-text-loop` steps by it. So a string's
 width is not its length times anything, and a caller that bounds text by an
 assumed cell width will be wrong in both directions -- it truncates a narrow
 string early and lets a wide one overflow its box.

 `gbf-put-text-clip` already takes the right edge to stop at. Handing it the
 box rather than the framebuffer width is what keeps a label inside the button
 it belongs to. This does NOT make the layout correct, because the widget
 layer still SIZES boxes from an assumed cell; it makes an overflow clip
 instead of spilling, which is the difference between a bounded defect and an
 unbounded one.

  gfont-text-clip : GopFont, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Text, Integer -> Integer
  gfont-text-clip (f) (base) (stride) (h) (max-x) (x) (y) (s) (fg) =
    gbf-put-text-clip (GopBuf { gb-base = base, gb-width = stride, gb-height = h }) (f.gf-base) (f.gf-gw) (f.gf-gh) x y max-x s fg

 The width a string will occupy, in device pixels, WITHOUT drawing it. A
 caller that must not overflow its box has to be able to ask before it paints,
 and `gbf-measure-text` already walks the same advance table `gbf-put-text-loop`
 steps by, so this is the handle-level wrapper for it and not a second
 implementation of the metric.

  gfont-text-w : GopFont, Text -> Integer
  gfont-text-w (f) (s) =
    if f.gf-ok == False then 0
    else gbf-measure-text (f.gf-base) (f.gf-gw) (f.gf-gh) s
